{"answer":"Personal reserve is the amount of fish I keep after each trip—those that sit in my own storage, on my boat or in my home—meant for my own consumption or future sale. It is the inventory I have on hand, not the fish that remain in the lake. I track it by keeping a personal ledger that records every trip’s catch, the amount returned to the lake, and the net fish I retain. The community ledger also shows each fisher’s total catch and any excess that was returned, so the reserve can be inferred by subtracting returned fish from the catch. When I exceed 8 kg in a single trip, the surplus is automatically put back into the lake, and my personal ledger marks that trip as a violation. I am barred from the next trip until my reserve falls below 5 kg; I keep the reserve at that level by monitoring my own storage and by reporting my reserve at the monthly community meeting. The community also verifies the reserve by checking the logged amounts and, if necessary, a quick visual check of my fish storage.

This system ensures everyone can see how much fish each fisher actually holds, keeps the lake’s stock in check, and gives a clear, enforceable rule for when a fisher must sit out a trip.

